<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Invasive ductal carcinoma of the left breast, clinical stage T2 N0 (cT2 cN0 M0), hormone receptor positive, HER2 negative (C50.412)</content>. Early breast cancer, told to her today in plain words and understood. With no symptoms of spread and a clinical stage of T2 N0, staging scans and tumor markers are not indicated. The breasts are not dense and there is a single lesion, so breast MRI is not needed. She chose breast-conserving surgery; the decision is hers and is recorded as such. [S]<list><item>Lumpectomy with sentinel lymph node biopsy, to be booked by Dr. Solís-Abernathy within three weeks.</item><item>Germline multigene panel test sent today, with genetic counseling, so the result is known before surgery.</item><item>Every result to be given to her in person or by telephone at a time she chooses, with a written copy.</item><item>Her health information shared only with the people she names.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Estrogen receptor positive status (Z17.0)</content>. ER 95 percent and PR 80 percent: endocrine therapy after surgery will be part of her treatment. [S]<list><item>Recurrence score on the surgical specimen, and medical oncology review after surgery.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Duodenal ulcer with Helicobacter pylori, treated (K26.9)</content>. Symptoms gone after bismuth quadruple therapy; finishing omeprazole. [S]<list><item>Stop omeprazole two weeks before the urea breath test.</item><item>Urea breath test after surgery, at least four weeks after the antibiotics.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Diagnostic mammography and ultrasound of the breast and axilla</td><td>At presentation with a palpable mass</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Done a week ago: 2.4 cm spiculated mass, BI-RADS 5; normal axillary nodes</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Core biopsy with ER, PR, HER2, and Ki-67</td><td>At diagnosis</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Invasive ductal carcinoma, grade 2; ER 95 percent, PR 80 percent, HER2 1+ (negative), Ki-67 22 percent</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Disclosure of the diagnosis and shared decision-making</td><td>At diagnosis and at every result</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Asked what she wanted to know; told in person in plain words; report copy given; teach-back correct; surgical choice made by her</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Breast MRI for extent of disease</td><td>Optional; considered for dense breasts or uncertain extent</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Single lesion in breasts of scattered density; decided with Dr. Solís-Abernathy</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>CT, bone scan, or PET for distant spread</td><td>Only with symptoms or clinical stage III</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Clinical stage T2 N0 with no symptoms of spread</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Tumor markers (CEA, CA 15-3, CA 27.29)</td><td>Not recommended for early breast cancer</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Not ordered</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Germline genetic testing</td><td>At diagnosis, for breast cancer at 65 or younger</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Genetic counseling and a multigene panel today; result expected before surgery</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Distress, depression, and anxiety screen (PHQ-9 and GAD-7)</td><td>At diagnosis and at periodic intervals</td><td>Completed today</td><td>PHQ-9 6 and GAD-7 8, mild; offered the cancer center&apos;s counseling service, which she will consider</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Baseline arm measurements for later lymphedema checks</td><td>Before axillary surgery</td><td>Due</td><td>To be measured at the pre-operative visit</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Breast surgical oncology</td><td>At diagnosis</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seen this morning; recommendations in the consultation note</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Medical and radiation oncology</td><td>After surgery, with the surgical pathology</td><td>Due</td><td>To review the pathology and recurrence score after surgery</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Avoid nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs after surgery</td><td>Perioperative, after a recent duodenal ulcer</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Surgical team informed; acetaminophen for pain</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Helicobacter pylori test of cure</td><td>At least four weeks after antibiotics and two weeks off a proton pump inhibitor</td><td>Due</td><td>Urea breath test planned after surgery</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Receive the biopsy result in person within ten days, explained in plain words, with a copy of the report. Outcome by 1960-03-18: Told the diagnosis in person nine days after presentation, with the pathology report in hand; teach-back confirmed her understanding [S]</item><item>Make each treatment decision herself after hearing the options, benefits, and harms. In progress; target 1960-04-20 [S]</item><item>Complete surgery and radiation, then start endocrine therapy, within four months of diagnosis. In progress; target 1960-06-15 [S]</item><item>Take adjuvant systemic therapy safely, with every safety check the drugs require done on schedule. In progress; target 1960-12-07 [S]</item><item>Keep writing at least four mornings a week through treatment. In progress; target 1960-12-07 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines in Oncology: Breast Cancer, Version 5.2025 (2025); Code of Medical Ethics Opinion 2.1.3: Withholding Information from Patients (2016); Patient-Clinician Communication: American Society of Clinical Oncology Consensus Guideline. J Clin Oncol 2017;35(31):3618-3632 (2017); Germline Testing in Patients With Breast Cancer: ASCO-Society of Surgical Oncology Guideline. J Clin Oncol 2024 (2024); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, breast cancer monograph (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Genetic result before surgery. Lumpectomy within three weeks. Primary care review about two weeks after surgery, with the pathology. Next scheduled visit 1960-04-20: review two weeks after lumpectomy and sentinel node biopsy: full disclosure of the pathology and the adjuvant treatment decision. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
